== 18. God’s Special Mark on Those Who Pray in Secret

Consider that God has set a special mark of favor, honor, and notice on those who have prayed in secret. You#super[+] may see this in Moses (Exod 34:28), and in Abraham (Gen 21:33), and in Isaac (Gen 24:63), and in Jacob (Gen 32:24–29), and in David (Ps 55:16–17), and in Daniel (Dan 6:10), and in Paul (Acts 9:11), and in Cornelius (Acts 10:2, 4), and in Peter (Acts 10:9–12), and in Manasseh (2 Chr 33:18–19). God has put on record all these worthies who have exercised themselves in secret prayer, to their everlasting fame and honor. The Persians seldom write their king’s name except in letters of gold. God has written, so to speak, in letters of gold the names of those who have made it a matter of conscience to exercise themselves in secret prayer. The precious names of those who have devoted themselves to closet duties are like statues of gold, which the polluted breath of men can never stain. They are like so many shining suns that no clouds can darken. They are like so many sparkling diamonds that shine brightest in the darkest night. A Christian can never get into a hole, a corner, a closet, to pour out his soul before the Lord without the Lord taking honorable notice of him and setting a secret mark of favor on him (Ezek 9:4–6). And how this should stir up all Christians to be much with God alone! The Romans were very ambitious to win a great name, a great report, in this world; why then should Christians not be as divinely ambitious to win a good name, a good report, in the other world (Heb 11:39)? A good name is always better than a great name, and a name in heaven is infinitely better than a thousand names on earth, and the way to both of these is to be much with God in secret.
